2 winning (4 melds + pair).  \n- Draw T4: then hand = B1 pung (4th meld) + T4 pair \u2192 winning.  \n\nSo you only win immediately if you draw W2 or T4, not B1.  \nSo B1\u00d73 is a pung candidate, not helping pair.  \n\nIf you discard B1 now: then hand = W2, T4, B1,B1 (B1 pair) \u2192 you need a meld from W2 and T4? They are unrelated, so you\u2019d need to draw tiles to make chow/pung with W2 or T4, which is more difficult.  \n\nTherefore, best is to keep B1\u00d73 and wait for W2 or T4.  \n\nNow, which tile to discard?  \nValid actions: Play B1, Play T4, Play W2.  \nIf you discard B1, you lose chance for pung and have B1 pair \u2014 but then you need a meld from W2/T4, harder.  \nIf you discard T4, you keep B1\u00d73 and W2 \u2014 then winning tiles = W2 (for pair) or T4? But T4 gone, so only W2 draw wins. Also if you draw B1, you have kong but no pair (only W2 single) \u2192 no win.  \nIf you discard W2, you keep B1\u00d73 and T4 \u2014 then winning tiles = T4 draw.  \n\nUnshown W2: 1 left, T4: 1 left. Equal chance. But look at opponents\u2019 discards:  \nNext player played W2 earlier (history: Play W2), across played none, previous played none.  \nT4: next player played T4 once, previous played T4 once.  \nSo both W2 and T4 have been seen, but not heavily.  \n\nGiven no major difference, keep both W2 and T4 to maximize winning draws. That means discard one B1. But discarding B1 worsens hand because you break pung and still need a meld from W2/T4. Wait \u2014 without pung, you have only 3 melds, need a 4th meld. W2 and T4 can\u2019t form meld alone unless you draw two specific tiles to form chow/pung with them, which is very unlikely in one draw.  \n\nSo discarding B1 is bad \u2014 you\u2019d need two more draws to complete hand.  \n\nThus discard either W2 or T4. Which one?  \nCheck potential patterns:  \n- If you keep T4, possible chow with T3,T4,T5 or T4,T5,T6 \u2014 but T5 unseen 0, T6 unseen 1, T3 unseen 1, so slim.  \n- If you keep W2, possible chow W1,W2,W3 \u2014 W1 unseen 3, W3 unseen 1, maybe possible.  \nBut you only need one tile to win \u2014 so chow possibilities irrelevant because you can\u2019t complete chow in one draw unless it\u2019s already 2 tiles waiting for 3rd.  \n\nActually, with B1\u00d73 and W2:  \nIf you draw W2 \u2192 win.  \nIf you draw B1 \u2192 no win.  \nIf you draw T4 \u2192 no win (you have T4 single, B1 pung, no pair).  \n\nWith B1\u00d73 and T4:  \nIf you draw T4 \u2192 win.  \nIf you draw B1 \u2192 no win.  \nIf you draw W2 \u2192 no win.  \n\nSo both give 1 winning tile type.  \n\nCheck opponents\u2019 exposures:  \nNext player meld [T6,T6,T6] reduces T6 count, [W3,W4,W5] reduces W3,W4,W5. Across has [T7,T7,T7]. Previous has [B8,B8,B8].  \nNo clear danger in discarding W2 or T4.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nKeeping B1\u00d73 is crucial because it\u2019s a ready pung to serve as 4th meld when you get a pair from W2 or T4. Discarding B1 leaves you with no easy 4th meld. Discarding W2 or T4 reduces winning tile types but you still have one winning tile type left.  \nSince W2 and T4 are symmetric in availability, choose to discard the one less useful for potential chows. T4 is less useful because T5 is gone (0 left), making T4 chow hard. W2 at least has W1 (3 left) and W3 (1 left) for chow, but again chow not needed for win now.  \nI\u2019ll choose to discard T4 to keep W2 because W2 may have slightly better tile connections if future draws change plan.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T4"}
